WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.039
Data control is the foundation of sovereign AI.

00:00:03.039 --> 00:00:07.081
An AI workflow may process prompts, uploaded files, retrieved documents, embeddings,

00:00:07.081 --> 00:00:10.755
chat history, generated outputs, tool results, logs, telemetry, evaluation records,

00:00:10.755 --> 00:00:11.857
and user feedback.

00:00:11.857 --> 00:00:14.429
Some of that data may be public.

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:15.899
Some may be internal.

00:00:15.899 --> 00:00:19.573
Some may be confidential, regulated, customer-specific, personal, or operationally sensitive.

00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:24.349
A practical program starts by classifying the data that can enter each workflow.

00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:29.125
Teams should know whether prompts may include customer records, source code, contract language,

00:00:29.125 --> 00:00:32.799
incident details, credentials, health information, financial data, or proprietary strategy.

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:35.004
They should also understand derived data.

00:00:35.004 --> 00:00:36.106
Embeddings, summaries, evaluations,

00:00:36.106 --> 00:00:41.984
and logs can carry sensitive meaning even when they do not look like the original document.

00:00:41.984 --> 00:00:44.189
Privacy boundaries can be broken accidentally.

00:00:44.189 --> 00:00:47.496
Sensitive prompts may be routed to the wrong service.

00:00:47.496 --> 00:00:49.700
Logs may be retained too long.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.639
Embeddings may be stored without a clear owner.

00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:54.844
Retrieval systems may ignore document permissions.

00:00:54.844 --> 00:00:59.620
Monitoring data may be shared more broadly than the underlying content would allow.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.294
These are design and operations problems, not only legal concerns.

00:01:03.294 --> 00:01:07.703
Sovereign AI needs explicit rules for what data can enter each workflow,

00:01:07.703 --> 00:01:11.010
where it can be stored, who can inspect it,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:16.153
how long it can be retained, and when it must be deleted or reviewed.

00:01:16.153 --> 00:01:19.460
Separate inference data from retrieval data and operational telemetry.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.297
Preserve tenant and permission boundaries.

00:01:21.297 --> 00:01:24.604
Use redaction and access control where logging is necessary.

00:01:24.604 --> 00:01:29.380
Strong data control makes later decisions about models, deployment, and policy much easier.
